WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.196
Mr. Fogg paid the cabman and was about to enter the train station when a poor beggar approached him.

2
00:00:07.197 --> 00:00:14.484
She was carrying a child in her arms, her bare feet were smeared with mud, and her clothes were old and torn.

3
00:00:14.485 --> 00:00:18.487
She asked Mr. Fogg for some money for food.

4
00:00:18.488 --> 00:00:25.546
Mr. Fogg took out the twenty guineas he had won that very evening at whist and handed them to her, saying, "Here, my good woman.

5
00:00:25.547 --> 00:00:30.405
I'm glad that I met you." Then he continued on his way.

6
00:00:30.406 --> 00:00:37.096
Passepartout was touched by his master's generosity, and he entered the station with a tear in his eye.

7
00:00:37.097 --> 00:00:41.154
Mr. Fogg quickly bought two first-class tickets for Paris.

8
00:00:41.155 --> 00:00:47.672
As he and Passepatout were crossing the platform, Mr. Fogg saw his five friends from the Reform Club.

9
00:00:47.673 --> 00:00:54.354
"Well, gentlemen," he said. "I'm off, and you can examine my passport when I get back.

10
00:00:54.355 --> 00:00:58.631
You will be able to judge then whether I completed the journey."

11
00:00:58.632 --> 00:01:03.725
"Oh, that is quite unnecessary, Mr. Fogg," said one man politely.

12
00:01:03.726 --> 00:01:07.768
"We trust your word as a gentleman of honor."

13
00:01:07.769 --> 00:01:12.610
"You do remember when you are due back in London?" asked another man.

14
00:01:12.611 --> 00:01:23.262
"In eighty days, on Saturday, December 21, 1872, at 8:45 p.m.," said Mr. Fogg. "Good-bye, gentlemen."

15
00:01:23.263 --> 00:01:30.919
And with that, Phileas Fogg and Passepartout boarded the train and seated themselves in the first-class carriage. Five-minutes

16
00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:36.464
later the train whistled, and then glided out of the station. Phileas

17
00:01:36.465 --> 00:01:44.402
Fogg suspected that his departure from London would create a lively sensation. He was right. The

18
00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:51.414
news of the extraordinary bet spread through the Reform Club and sparked excited conversation among its members. The

19
00:01:51.415 --> 00:01:58.656
news quickly got into the newspapers around London, and the "tour of the world" was soon being talked about all over town. Some

20
00:01:58.657 --> 00:02:03.991
people supported Mr. Fogg and believed his journey would be a success. But

21
00:02:03.992 --> 00:02:11.012
many thought he was insane for betting so much money on a journey he could not possibly complete in eighty days. Many

22
00:02:11.013 --> 00:02:15.676
of the newspapers claimed Mr. Fogg's project was madness. Only

23
00:02:15.677 --> 00:02:19.629
the Daily Telegraph supported his adventure. Soon

24
00:02:19.630 --> 00:02:25.783
the members of the Reform Club weren't the only ones to place wagers on Phileas Fogg's journey. His

25
00:02:25.784 --> 00:02:30.628
name began appearing in the betting books as if he were a racehorse! Five

26
00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:37.119
days after his departure, an article appeared in the bulletin of the Royal Geographical Society. Everything,

27
00:02:37.120 --> 00:02:41.124
it said, was against the travelers. There

28
00:02:41.125 --> 00:02:45.476
would be obstacles imposed by humans and by nature. Furthermore,

29
00:02:45.477 --> 00:02:52.546
Fogg's success would depend on a miraculous agreement of trains and steamers' departure and arrival times. Mr.

30
00:02:52.547 --> 00:02:59.772
Fogg might be able to depend on trains arriving on time in Europe, where distances were relatively short. But

31
00:02:59.773 --> 00:03:07.452
when he was trying to cross India in three days, and the United States in seven, transportation would not be as reliable. Surely

32
00:03:07.453 --> 00:03:14.933
there would be problems with machinery, trains running off the tracks, collisions, and bad weather. Still,

33
00:03:14.934 --> 00:03:20.866
as Phileas Fogg rode the train out of London, he was relaxed and confident in his first-class carriage, unconcerned

34
00:03:20.867 --> 00:03:24.833
about any of the problems that could interrupt his journey. Passepartout,

35
00:03:24.834 --> 00:03:31.479
however, had still not recovered from the shock of being whisked away on such short notice. He

36
00:03:31.480 --> 00:03:38.313
clung mechanically to the carpetbag filled with money and stared straight ahead without seeing a thing. As

37
00:03:38.314 --> 00:03:46.484
the train was speeding through Sydenham, just outside London, Passepartout suddenly uttered a cry of despair. "What's

38
00:03:46.485 --> 00:03:49.855
the matter?" asked Mr. Fogg. "Alas!"

39
00:03:49.856 --> 00:03:55.392
cried Passepartout. "In my hurry I forgot . . ." "What?"

40
00:03:55.393 --> 00:03:57.553
asked Mr. Fogg. "To

41
00:03:57.554 --> 00:04:01.607
turn off the gas in my room!" said Passepartout. "Very

42
00:04:01.608 --> 00:04:08.597
well, young man," returned Mr. Fogg coolly. "It will burn . . . at your expense." Passepartout

43
00:04:08.598 --> 00:04:14.214
looked down in embarrassment. It was his fault, after all. After

44
00:04:14.215 --> 00:04:20.698
arriving in Calais, they traveled by train to Paris and then through the Alps to Turin in northern Italy. They

45
00:04:20.699 --> 00:04:33.269
traversed Italy to Brindisi, at the heel of Italy's boot, where they boarded the ship Mongolia and sailed for Suez. Passepartout's

46
00:04:33.270 --> 00:04:39.884
Guidebook So Mr. Fogg and I had just caught the train from Paris, France, to Brindisi, a port in the south of Italy.

47
00:04:39.885 --> 00:04:44.681
What a long trip it was! Although I must say, it was extremely comfortable.

48
00:04:44.682 --> 00:04:49.324
After all, this is known as the "golden age" of rail travel.

49
00:04:49.325 --> 00:04:53.467
Train travel is now more popular and comfortable than ever before.

50
00:04:53.468 --> 00:05:00.496
Train tracks first appeared in Europe in the 1500s, but the "train" was a wagon pulled along the track by horses.

51
00:05:00.497 --> 00:05:06.968
In 1804 an English steam engine pulled train cars with passengers for the first time.

52
00:05:06.969 --> 00:05:10.730
Early steam engines like this were often called "iron horses."

53
00:05:10.731 --> 00:05:16.576
I'm just glad I didn't have to "feed" it the coal it needed! What a tough and dirty job!

54
00:05:16.577 --> 00:05:19.623
The first passenger trains were pretty rough.

55
00:05:19.624 --> 00:05:28.977
They seldom traveled more than 18 miles per hour, passengers sat on wooden benches, and meals were eaten quickly in dining halls at train stations.

56
00:05:28.978 --> 00:05:32.510
Thankfully, train travel has improved a lot since then.

57
00:05:32.511 --> 00:05:39.051
By the 1860s trains could travel at around 99 miles per hour, and no more wooden benches!

58
00:05:39.052 --> 00:05:43.170
Passengers now have a choice of first, second, or third class.

59
00:05:43.171 --> 00:05:46.428
First class is like a hotel on wheels!

60
00:05:46.429 --> 00:05:51.951
The rooms are large and luxurious, and the dining car resembles a fancy restaurant.

61
00:05:51.952 --> 00:06:00.793
There are also bars and lounges where passengers can relax, and watch the countryside go by.

